    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-121717",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time",
      "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{4t+1},3t-2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4t+1}},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4(2)+1}},3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}},3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
