    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-121730",
      "knowledge_point_id": "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time",
      "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=\\pi .$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\pi ,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\pi ) & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨-4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=\\pi $ we calculate the magnitude of $v(\\pi ),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(\\pi )| & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=\\pi $ is $4.$",
