    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-121739",
      "knowledge_point_id": "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{2}-6t,(t-3)^{5}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-6t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-3)^{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-6,5(t-3)^{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(t-3),5(t-3)^{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2(t-3),5(t-3)^{4}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=3, \\\\ 5(t-3)^{4}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=3$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary.",
