    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-352611",
      "knowledge_point_id": "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+1},\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+5}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$  \n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,3(t-2)(t+4)=0\\,⇒\\,t=2,-4, \\\\ \\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,3(t-2)(t+12)=0\\,⇒\\,t=2,-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=2$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary.",
